\chapter{Feature Tags and Mixins: Anatomy of Composition}
\label{ch:feature-tags}

\section{The Feature Protocol}

\DSLtk{} has exactly one extension point, and it is a small one. A feature
is a tag: an empty struct that may contain a nested template named
\code{Mixin}. The tag is what you name in the feature list; the mixin is
what the library instantiates with your type and inherits into your class.
Listing~\ref{lst:ch06-protocol} shows the protocol in its minimal form.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The feature protocol: a tag with a nested \code{Mixin}, and a DSL that names it.},label={lst:ch06-protocol}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <string_view>

namespace mydsl {

/// A feature: the tag names it, the mixin delivers it.
struct Greeting
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    /// A static member: reached through the class name.
    static constexpr std::string_view
    salutation ()
    {
      return "hello";
    }
  };
};

} // namespace mydsl

struct Greeter : dsl::DSL<Greeter, mydsl::Greeting>
{
};

int main ()
{
  std::cout << Greeter::salutation () << "\n";
}
\end{lstlisting}

Three properties of this protocol are worth stating before the details.

The tag is \emph{nothing but a name}. It has no data, no behaviour, no
registration step, and no relationship to any other tag. Two tags are
unrelated types, and the library never asks whether a tag is ``known''.

The mixin is parameterised by the deriving type, and only by the deriving
type. This is what lets a feature reach into your class, and it is what
makes the mixin safe to reuse across every DSL that names it.

The mixin may contribute members of four different kinds -- static
functions, instance functions, operators, and (implicitly) nothing at all.
Chapter~\ref{ch:architecture} distinguished the seven features that
contribute members from the nineteen that do not; this chapter takes the
seven apart.

\section{The Anatomy of a Mixin}

\subsection{The Tag}

A tag is declared at namespace scope. It may be empty, and in the shipped
header every tag is empty except for the nested mixin. The tag's name is
what appears in the feature list, and the tag's \emph{type} is what the
library uses to find the mixin: the base clause
\code{Features::template Mixin<Derived>\ldots} names the member template
\code{Mixin} of the tag.

The consequence is that a tag without a nested \code{Mixin} template is
ill-formed, and the diagnostic appears where the DSL is declared rather than
where the tag is:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={A tag with no nested \code{Mixin}, and the diagnostic it produces at the point of use.},label={lst:ch06-nomixin}]
error: no class template named 'Mixin' in 'struct NoMixin'
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{The Nested Mixin}

The mixin is a class template with exactly one parameter, the deriving type.
It is instantiated once per DSL that names the feature, and the
instantiation becomes a base class of \code{dsl::DSL}. Because the mixin is
a base of \code{dsl::DSL}, and \code{dsl::DSL} is a base of your class, the
mixin is a base of your class -- which is exactly what the
\code{HasFeature} concept of Chapter~\ref{ch:crtp-foundation} tests.

The mixin may be as simple as this:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The no-op mixin. Nineteen of the shipped feature tags are exactly this, and they are the marker features.},label={lst:ch06-noop}]
struct PatternMatch
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    // Feature presence marker; no additional methods needed since
    // dsl::match/when/otherwise are free functions in the dsl:: namespace.
  };
};
\end{lstlisting}

The no-op mixin is not a stub or an oversight. It is the declaration that
the feature exists and that it is available as free functions. A DSL that
names \code{dsl::PatternMatch} is declaring that it is a pattern-matching
DSL; the concept \code{HasFeature<T, PatternMatch>} then answers that
question for any generic code.

\subsection{The Constraint Idiom}

Two of the shipped mixins constrain themselves on a member of the deriving
class. The idiom is a nested requirement:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The \code{requires requires} idiom, taken from \code{Rewrite}'s mixin and shown as it appears inside \code{namespace dsl}. The inner requirement asks whether \code{Derived::rules} is well formed; the outer clause constrains the member on the answer.},label={lst:ch06-constraint}]
struct Rewrite
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    ASTNode
    rewrite (const ASTNode &tree) const
      requires requires { Derived::rules; }
    {
      return Derived::rules.apply (tree);
    }
  };
};
\end{lstlisting}

There are two requirements here, and they do different jobs. The inner one
is a \emph{requirement}: an expression that must be well formed for the
constraint to be satisfied. \code{Derived::rules} is well formed exactly
when the deriving class declares a member named \code{rules}; because
\code{Derived} is a dependent type, that question cannot be answered until
the mixin is instantiated, which is why it has to be written as a
requirement rather than as a plain expression.

The outer one is a \emph{constraint}: a boolean condition on the member. If
the deriving class has no \code{rules}, the constraint is not satisfied and
the member \code{rewrite} does not exist for that class. Calling it produces
the diagnostic \emph{``constraints not satisfied''}, which names the
problem directly instead of reporting a missing member.

The pattern is repeated verbatim in \code{CustomLiterals}' mixin, with
\code{Derived::literals} in place of \code{Derived::rules}.

\paragraph{A constraint is not the same as a declaration.}
\code{Memoization}'s mixin is unconstrained, even though its
\code{clear_cache} needs a \code{memo_cache} member:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={\code{Memoization}'s mixin, unconstrained. The requirement on \code{Derived::memo_cache} is enforced only when the member is instantiated, that is, when it is called.},label={lst:ch06-unconstrained}]
struct Memoization
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    /// Clears all cached entries stored in `Derived::memo_cache`.
    void
    clear_cache ()
    {
      static_cast<Derived &> (*this).memo_cache.clear ();
    }
  };
};
\end{lstlisting}

Because a member function of a class template is instantiated only when it
is used, a DSL that names \code{dsl::Memoization} but does not declare
\code{memo_cache} compiles perfectly well -- as long as nobody calls
\code{clear_cache()}. The error appears at the call site instead, and it
reads like an ordinary missing-member diagnostic. The constrained form is
better; the shipped code is inconsistent about which it uses, and a custom
feature should prefer the constrained form.

\section{Four Kinds of Contribution}

\subsection{Static Members}

A static member is found through the class name and needs no instance.
\code{Pipeline}, \code{Operators}, \code{AST} and \code{CustomLiterals} all
contribute static members. Listing~\ref{lst:ch06-static} shows
\code{AST}'s two.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Static members: \code{AST}'s \code{make_leaf} and \code{make_node}, transcribed from the header at lines 6723--6748 and shown as they appear inside \code{namespace dsl}.},label={lst:ch06-static}]
struct AST
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    template <FixedString Tag, typename T>
    static ASTNode
    make_leaf (T &&val)
    {
      return dsl::leaf<Tag> (std::forward<T> (val));
    }

    template <FixedString Tag, typename... Children>
      requires (std::convertible_to<Children, ASTNode> && ...)
    static ASTNode
    make_node (Children &&...children)
    {
      return dsl::node<Tag> (std::forward<Children> (children)...);
    }
  };
};
\end{lstlisting}

Note that neither member mentions \code{Derived}. A static member that
needed the deriving type would have to name it explicitly, and the shipped
mixins that do need it -- \code{Rewrite}, \code{CustomLiterals},
\code{Memoization} -- are the ones that reach for it.

\subsection{Instance Members}

An instance member acts on per-object state, and therefore needs the object.
\code{Rewrite}'s \code{rewrite} is an instance member, and so is
\code{Memoization}'s \code{clear_cache}. The former reads a static member
(\code{Derived::rules}) but is still an instance member, because the
library's designers chose to make it callable on an instance;
\code{clear_cache} is an instance member because the cache belongs to the
object.

\subsection{Operators, Injected as Hidden Friends}

The third kind of contribution is the interesting one, and it is the
mechanism that lets a DSL have its own operators without polluting the
global namespace. \code{ExprTemplates} is the shipped example.
Listing~\ref{lst:ch06-hidden} is its mixin in full, transcribed from lines
7056--7099.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={\code{ExprTemplates}' mixin, shown as it appears inside \code{namespace dsl}. The five operators are \emph{hidden friends}: friend functions defined inside the class, findable only through argument-dependent lookup.},label={lst:ch06-hidden}]
struct ExprTemplates
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    /// operator+ (binary)
    template <typename R>
    friend constexpr auto
    operator+ (const Derived &lhs, const R &rhs)
    {
      return BinExpr<std::plus<>, Derived, R>{ lhs, rhs };
    }

    /// operator- (binary)
    template <typename R>
    friend constexpr auto
    operator- (const Derived &lhs, const R &rhs)
    {
      return BinExpr<std::minus<>, Derived, R>{ lhs, rhs };
    }

    /// operator* (binary)
    template <typename R>
    friend constexpr auto
    operator* (const Derived &lhs, const R &rhs)
    {
      return BinExpr<std::multiplies<>, Derived, R>{ lhs, rhs };
    }

    /// operator/ (binary)
    template <typename R>
    friend constexpr auto
    operator/ (const Derived &lhs, const R &rhs)
    {
      return BinExpr<std::divides<>, Derived, R>{ lhs, rhs };
    }

    /// Unary negation
    friend constexpr auto
    operator- (const Derived &e)
    {
      return UnaryExpr<std::negate<>, Derived>{ e };
    }
  };
};
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Free Functions}

The fourth kind of contribution is not a contribution at all. Nineteen of
the shipped features contribute only free functions in namespace
\code{dsl}: \code{dsl::match}, \code{dsl::when}, \code{dsl::otherwise},
\code{dsl::leaf}, \code{dsl::node}, \code{dsl::rule},
\code{dsl::rewrite_set}, \code{dsl::lit}, \code{dsl::literal_set},
\code{dsl::parser}, \code{dsl::ch}, \code{dsl::satisfy},
\code{dsl::run_parser}, \code{dsl::create_peg_definition},
\code{dsl::compile_visitor}, \code{dsl::make_cfg} and the rest. A marker
feature is exactly a feature whose facilities live at namespace scope.

\section{How Hidden Friends Are Found}

This is the subtlest mechanism in the library, so it is worth setting it out
step by step.

A \emph{hidden friend} is a friend function defined inside a class. It has
no namespace scope of its own: it cannot be found by ordinary unqualified
lookup, and it does not appear in any overload set except the ones that
argument-dependent lookup constructs. The only way to find it is for one of
its parameters to have an associated class that encloses the friend.

In \code{ExprTemplates::Mixin<Derived>}, the friend
\code{operator+(const Derived&, const R&)} is declared inside the class
\code{ExprTemplates::Mixin<Derived>}. The class in which it is declared is
therefore an associated class of the friend.

Now consider the call \code{a + b}, where \code{a} has type
\code{Scalar} and \code{Scalar} derives from
\code{dsl::DSL<Scalar, ExprTemplates>}, which in turn derives from
\code{ExprTemplates::Mixin<Scalar>}. The associated classes of the call
include every base class of \code{Scalar}, and therefore include
\code{ExprTemplates::Mixin<Scalar>}. Argument-dependent lookup searches the
scope of each associated class for friends declared in it, finds the hidden
friend, and selects it.

That is the whole trick. The operator becomes visible exactly when one of
its operands is the deriving type, and at no other time.

\subsection{Why Not a Namespace-Scope Operator?}

The obvious alternative is a free function at namespace scope:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The design that was not used: a namespace-scope operator template over the deriving type.},label={lst:ch06-freeop}]
template <typename Derived, typename R>
constexpr auto
operator+ (const Derived &lhs, const R &rhs)
{
  return BinExpr<std::plus<>, Derived, R>{ lhs, rhs };
}
\end{lstlisting}

This does not work, and the reason is instructive. A namespace-scope
operator template participates in ordinary unqualified lookup and in
argument-dependent lookup for \emph{every} call of the form \code{x + y} in
the entire program. The compiler must therefore attempt to deduce
\code{Derived} from the left operand of every addition anywhere, including
\code{int + int}, \code{std::string + std::string} and
\code{double + double}. Each of those attempts fails to deduce
\code{Derived}, and the resulting error messages are bewildering: a user
adding two integers would be told that no matching \code{operator+} exists,
with a note about \code{Derived} being unable to be deduced.

The hidden friend avoids the problem entirely, because the friend is not
visible during ordinary lookup at all. It is found only if argument-dependent
lookup reaches it, and argument-dependent lookup reaches it only when an
operand has the right associated class.

\subsection{A Trace of the Lookup}

It is worth following the lookup for a single expression, because the
mechanism is easy to describe and easy to get wrong. The procedure below
is what the compiler does for \code{a + b}, where \code{a} is a
\code{Scalar} and \code{b} is a \code{double}.

\begin{pseudocode}[Finding a hidden friend by argument-dependent lookup]
\textbf{function} adl (call)\\
\quad candidates \leftarrow \{\}\\
\quad \textbf{for} each argument \code{arg} of the call\\
\qquad \textbf{for} each class \code{C} associated with \code{arg}\\
\qquad\qquad \textbf{for} each base class \code{B} of \code{C}\\
\qquad\qquad \qquad \textbf{for} each friend declared in \code{B}\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad \textbf{if} the friend is a candidate for the call\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad \qquad candidates \leftarrow candidates \texttt{ union } \{\code{friend}\}\\
\quad \textbf{return} candidates, to be resolved with the ordinary
  \qquad\quad overload rules together with ordinary lookup
\end{pseudocode}

The associated classes of the left argument \code{a} are \code{Scalar} and
all of its base classes. The base classes are \code{dsl::DSL<Scalar,
ExprTemplates>}, then \code{ExprTemplates::Mixin<Scalar>}. The friend
\code{operator+} is declared in that last class, so it is a candidate. The
right argument \code{b}, a \code{double}, contributes nothing.

The candidate set therefore contains exactly one hidden friend. Ordinary
unqualified lookup, which searches the enclosing namespaces, contributes
nothing at all, because a friend declared inside a class is not in any
namespace.

The final step is ordinary overload resolution over the union of the two
sets. If the user has also written a namespace-scope
\code{operator+(const Scalar&, double)} in their own namespace, that
function is found by ordinary lookup, and the two candidates are then
resolved by the usual rules -- which is to say that the outcome depends on
the relative order of the two declarations, exactly as it would for any
pair of operators.

\subsection{The Left-Operand Asymmetry}\label{sec:ch06-left}

There is a consequence of the friend signature that is easy to miss. The
binary friends take \code{const Derived&} as their \emph{first}
parameter, so the deriving type must be on the left:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={The asymmetry, in two lines of output. \code{a + b} compiles; \code{b + a} does not.},label={lst:ch06-asym}]
two + 3.0        // fine: the hidden friend's first parameter is Derived
3.0 + two        // error: no match for 'operator+' (operand types are
                 //        'double' and 'Scalar')
\end{lstlisting}

The library patches this for the expression-template tree itself. Lines
7103--7129 declare four namespace-scope operators whose first parameter is
a \code{BinExpr}, so that \code{(a + b) * c} works with the tree on the
left. Those operators are safe at namespace scope precisely because their
first parameter is a concrete type -- \code{BinExpr} -- so they never
compete with anything else.

\subsection{What the Operator Does Not Check}

\code{ExprTemplates}' operators are unconstrained. They call
\code{lhs.expr_value()} without requiring that the deriving class has such
a member. A DSL that names \code{dsl::ExprTemplates} but does not provide
\code{expr_value()} compiles until the operator is used, at which point the
error is an ordinary missing-member diagnostic.

The library's own concept \code{ExprLike} does check it properly, by
requiring \code{t.expr_value()} on a \code{const T&}. A custom feature
that injects an operator should do the same.

\section{Injecting a Type, Not Just a Member}

Everything said so far has been about member functions, but a mixin may
contribute anything a class can contain: a nested type, a type alias, a
static data member, a constant. This is the natural way to give a DSL its
own vocabulary types.

Listing~\ref{lst:ch06-type} defines a feature that contributes a nested
struct and a type alias.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A feature that injects a type. Because the mixin is a base class of the deriving type, the nested type is reachable as \code{S::Tag} and the alias as \code{S::self_type}.},label={lst:ch06-type}]
namespace mydsl {

struct Tagged
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    /// A nested type: part of the DSL's vocabulary.
    struct Tag
    {
      static constexpr std::string_view text = "tagged";
    };

    /// An alias for the deriving type, available to user code.
    using self_type = Derived;
  };
};

} // namespace mydsl

struct S : dsl::DSL<S, mydsl::Tagged> {};

// S::Tag::text is "tagged"
// std::is_same_v<S::self_type, S> is true
\end{lstlisting}

Two features of this are worth noting. The nested type is a member of the
mixin, and the mixin is a base of \code{S}, so the type is inherited and
reachable through the derived class's name. And the alias
\code{using self_type = Derived;} is the mixin's own use of its template
parameter: the mixin knows the deriving type, and can publish it.

Note also that these contributions do not interact with the concept of
Chapter~\ref{ch:crtp-foundation} in any special way. \code{HasFeature<S,
mydsl::Tagged>} is true because \code{mydsl::Tagged::Mixin<S>} is a base of
\code{S}; the nested type and the alias are simply inherited.

\subsection{What a Mixin Cannot Contribute}

A mixin cannot contribute a constructor that takes the deriving class's
arguments, because the mixin is instantiated as a base and never
constructed in its own right. It cannot contribute a destructor, for the
same reason. It cannot be final, because it is inherited. And it cannot
contribute a virtual function usefully, because there is no virtual
dispatch anywhere in the arrangement -- Chapter~\ref{ch:crtp-foundation}
explains why.

\section{How Features Interact}

Features are independent by default. Naming \code{dsl::Rewrite} does not
require \code{dsl::AST}, because \code{Rewrite}'s mixin works with the free
type \code{dsl::ASTNode} rather than with anything the \code{AST} mixin
provides. A DSL that names only \code{dsl::Rewrite} and declares a
\code{rules} member compiles and its \code{rewrite} method works.

The dependencies that do exist run through the deriving class, not through
the feature list:

\begin{itemize}
\item \code{Rewrite} needs \code{Derived::rules}, a static member.
\item \code{CustomLiterals} needs \code{Derived::literals}, a static member.
\item \code{Memoization} needs \code{Derived::memo_cache}, an instance
      member.
\item \code{ExprTemplates} needs \code{Derived::expr_value()}, an instance
      member, but does not check for it.
\end{itemize}

When a feature genuinely needs another feature -- because the member it
provides is meaningless without the other -- the way to say so is the
\code{HasFeature} concept. A constrained member is the mechanism:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A feature that requires another feature. The constraint is checked at the point of use, and the diagnostic names the constraint.},label={lst:ch06-requires}]
namespace mydsl {

/// Feature tag: requires the AST feature and adds an instance member.
struct Simplify
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    dsl::ASTNode
    simplify (const dsl::ASTNode &tree) const
      requires dsl::HasFeature<Derived, dsl::AST>
    {
      return dsl::node<"simplified"> (tree);
    }
  };
};

} // namespace mydsl
\end{lstlisting}

A DSL that names \code{mydsl::Simplify} without \code{dsl::AST} compiles,
but calling \code{simplify} does not:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={The diagnostic produced by a violated feature-on-feature constraint.},label={lst:ch06-violated}]
error: no matching function for call to 'Bad::simplify(dsl::ASTNode)'
note: candidate: 'dsl::ASTNode mydsl::Simplify::Mixin<Derived>::simplify(...)
       constraints not satisfied
\end{lstlisting}

\section{A Feature That Injects an Operator}

Listing~\ref{lst:ch06-custom} is a complete program that defines two
custom features: one that injects an operator as a hidden friend, and one
that requires an existing feature. It uses the library's own
\code{AST} feature, the library's free functions, and the
\code{HasFeature} concept.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Two custom feature tags: one injecting a hidden-friend operator, one requiring an existing feature.},label={lst:ch06-custom}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <iostream>

namespace mydsl {

/// Feature tag: injects `operator^` (integer power) as a hidden friend.
struct Power
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    template <typename R>
    friend constexpr auto
    operator^ (const Derived &lhs, const R &rhs)
    {
      double base = lhs.expr_value ();
      double exponent = static_cast<double> (rhs);
      double result = 1.0;
      for (int step = 0; step < static_cast<int> (exponent); ++step)
        result *= base;
      return result;
    }
  };
};

/// Feature tag: requires the AST feature and adds an instance member.
struct Simplify
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    dsl::ASTNode
    simplify (const dsl::ASTNode &tree) const
      requires dsl::HasFeature<Derived, dsl::AST>
    {
      return dsl::node<"simplified"> (tree);
    }
  };
};

} // namespace mydsl

struct Number : dsl::DSL<Number, mydsl::Power, dsl::AST, mydsl::Simplify>
{
  double value{};
  Number () = default;
  explicit Number (double v) : value (v) {}
  double expr_value () const { return value; }
};

int main ()
{
  Number two{2.0};
  auto eighth = two ^ 8;
  std::cout << "two ^ 8 = " << eighth << "\n";

  auto tree = Number::make_leaf<"num"> (7);
  Number instance;
  std::cout << "simplified = " << instance.simplify (tree).dump () << "\n";
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output is

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch06-custom}.},label={lst:ch06-custom-out}]
two ^ 8 = 256
simplified = (simplified (num 7))
\end{lstlisting}

The operator \code{\^} is worth a second look. It is a hidden friend
declared inside \code{mydsl::Power::Mixin<Number>}, so it is found by
argument-dependent lookup when the left operand is a \code{Number}, and it
is invisible everywhere else. No other program that includes this header
sees it, and no \code{operator^} in the global namespace has been
polluted. Note also that \code{\^} has lower precedence than \code{+}, so
\code{two ^ 8 + 1} parses as \code{two ^ (8 + 1)} -- a reminder that
injected operators come with the precedence of the operator symbol they
reuse.

\section{Mixin Assembly}

The diagram below shows how a feature list becomes an object.
The procedure below states the same process in words.

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\begin{dslgraph}
  \node[dslbox, text width=42mm] (m1) at (-4.6,1.5) {\code{Pipeline::Mixin<Greeter>}};
  \node[dslbox, text width=42mm] (m2) at (-4.6,0) {\code{AST::Mixin<Greeter>}};
  \node[dslbox, text width=42mm] (m3) at (-4.6,-1.5) {\code{Rewrite::Mixin<Greeter>}};

  \node[dslbox, text width=48mm, minimum height=13mm] (base) at (0,0) {\code{dsl::DSL<Greeter, Pipeline, AST, Rewrite>}\\three direct bases, no data};

  \node[dslbox, text width=38mm] (derived) at (4.8,0) {\code{struct Greeter :}\\ \code{dsl::DSL<...>}};

  \draw[dsledge] (m1) -- (base);
  \draw[dsledge] (m2) -- (base);
  \draw[dsledge] (m3) -- (base);
  \draw[dsledge] (base) -- (derived);
  \draw[dslfeedback] (derived.south) -- ++(0,-8mm) -| node[pos=0.25, below, font=\scriptsize] {the mixins are bases of the base} (m3.south);
\end{dslgraph}
\caption{Mixin assembly. Each feature tag contributes one base class to
\code{dsl::DSL}; the deriving class inherits from that, and therefore from
all of them. The arrow back to the mixins records that the derivation is
direct: \code{Rewrite::Mixin<Greeter>} is a base of \code{Greeter}.}
\label{fig:ch06-assembly}
\end{figure}

\begin{pseudocode}[Assembling a DSL from a feature list]
\textbf{function} assemble (Derived, F1, F2, \ldots, Fn)\\
\quad \textbf{for} each feature \code{Fi}\\
\qquad \textbf{require} \code{Fi::Mixin} exists\\
\qquad instantiate \code{Mi = Fi::Mixin<Derived>}\\
\qquad \textbf{if} \code{Mi} declares members then\\
\qquad\qquad \textbf{comment} those members are inherited by \code{Derived}\\
\qquad \textbf{else}\\
\qquad\qquad \textbf{comment} \code{Mi} is a marker: no members, no cost\\
\quad \textbf{return} \code{Derived}, which inherits from every \code{Mi}
\end{pseudocode}

\section{The Same Extension Point in Both Ports}

The C++ and D ports agree on the shape of a custom feature, even though they
disagree on almost everything else about composition. In D, the
\code{DSL!} mixin template ends with a loop that mixes in any feature's own
nested mixin:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={The D port's custom-feature extension point, from \code{DSLtk.d} lines 117--120.},label={lst:ch06-dmixin2}]
static foreach (Feature; Features)
{
    static if (__traits(hasMember, Feature, "Mixin")) mixin Feature.Mixin;
}
\end{lstlisting}

So a D feature tag may also carry a nested \code{Mixin}, and that mixin is
mixed into the deriving struct. The D module header says so explicitly:
\emph{``Custom feature tags may supply a nested mixin template named
Mixin.''} The D port's own test suite exercises this, with a tag that
declares \code{mixin template Mixin() { int custom_value() const { return
17; } }}.

The C++ port requires the nested \code{Mixin} unconditionally: the base
clause names it, so a tag without one does not compile. The D port makes it
optional, because the D \code{DSL!} template supplies its own members for
the built-in features and only reaches for \code{Feature.Mixin} when the
feature provides one.

The practical difference for a user writing a custom feature is small. In
both ports you write a tag with a nested mixin; in C++ the mixin is a
class template parameterised by the deriving type, and in D it is a mixin
template with no parameters, mixed into the enclosing struct.

