\section{Edge Cases and Limits}

\paragraph{A feature tag must contain a nested \code{Mixin}.}
There is no default. The library does not provide a fallback mixin, so a tag
without one is a compile error at the point where the DSL is declared.

\paragraph{The mixin takes exactly one parameter.}
The expansion is \code{F::Mixin<Derived>}. A mixin with a second template
parameter, or with a defaulted one that the library does not supply, will
not instantiate.

\paragraph{The mixin is instantiated once per deriving type.}
A feature used by ten DSLs is instantiated ten times, and each
instantiation is a distinct type. This is why the mixins are thin: the cost
of instantiating them is multiplied by the number of DSLs that use them.

\paragraph{Feature-to-feature constraints are unusual.}
Only two of the shipped mixins constrain themselves on another feature, and
neither does: both constrain themselves on a member of the deriving class.
If your feature truly needs another feature, use
\code{requires dsl::HasFeature<Derived, OtherFeature>}, and be aware that
this makes the feature order in the list irrelevant while making the
dependency explicit in the source.

\paragraph{Hidden friends are found only on the left for binary operators.}
As Section~\ref{sec:ch06-left} showed, \code{b + a} does not compile when
\code{a} is the deriving type and \code{b} is not. If your language needs
the deriving type on either side, you must declare two friends.

\paragraph{Hidden friends do not participate in overload resolution against
ordinary candidates in the way you might expect.}
They are found by argument-dependent lookup, which means they are considered
for arguments of the deriving type and are invisible otherwise. A user's
\code{operator+(const MyType&, const MyType&)} at namespace scope will
still be found by ordinary lookup for \code{MyType + MyType}, and the two
candidates will be resolved by the usual overload rules. If both are
viable, the outcome depends on the relative ordering of the two
declarations, exactly as with any pair of operators.

\paragraph{An injected operator inherits the precedence and associativity of
the symbol it reuses.}
\code{\^} binds more tightly than \code{+}; \code{<<} binds more tightly
than \code{+}. A language that wants different precedences must not reuse
those symbols.

\paragraph{Marker features cost nothing but also do nothing.}
Nineteen of the twenty-six tags contribute no members. A marker in a feature
list is documentation and a queryable fact, not a capability.

\paragraph{The feature list is fixed at compile time.}
There is no way to add a feature to an existing DSL type, and no runtime
mechanism for choosing between feature sets. The pack is part of the type.

\section{What Comes Next}

The composition mechanism is now fully described: the tag, the mixin, the
four kinds of contribution, the hidden-friend technique, and the
interactions between features. The next chapter opens the first of the
standalone subsystems, the enum-reflection facility that the library
inherits from the embedded \code{magic_enum} v0.9.8.
